WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.100
PRESENTER: I am pleased
to welcome to the platform

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.060
the Honorable Francis H. Duehay,
Mayor of the City of Cambridge.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.630
It is also my pleasure to
welcome Raymond Magliozzi,

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:36.660
class of 1972, and Thomas
L. Magliozzi, class of '58,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.360
hosts of the National Public
Radio series Car Talk.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:51.448
The Magliozzi brothers will now
give the commencement address.

00:00:51.448 --> 00:00:53.943
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:59.398 --> 00:01:00.940
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Don't crowd me.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.815
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Glad you could all come.

00:01:02.815 --> 00:01:04.620
[BLOWHORN]

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.570
Shut up.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:07.860
[BLOWHORN]

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.595
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
What's this?

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:11.095
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Wait a minute.

00:01:11.095 --> 00:01:13.338
I have to put my glasses
on and everything.

00:01:13.338 --> 00:01:16.463
You're not supposed to start.

00:01:16.463 --> 00:01:17.880
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
When this was

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.840
announced in the newspaper--

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.630
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
This happens all the time.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.960
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: This was
announced in the newspaper.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.510
And my daughter-- my
lovely daughter Lydia,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.980
who's sitting over there--

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.620
called me and
said, is this true?

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.650
And I said, yeah.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.940
She said, when is commencement?

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.910
I said, it's June the 4th.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.150
She said, promise
me just one thing.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.005
I said, what?

00:01:40.005 --> 00:01:43.800
She says, promise me that you'll
think about it before June 3.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.630
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.780
Which reminded me of that
great old country music

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.420
song, "How Come You
Know Me So Good When

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.216
I'm a Stranger to Myself."

00:01:54.216 --> 00:01:56.560
Now, what do you want to say?

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.530
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, not much actually.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.720
I just wanted to
say, we are thrilled

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.610
to be here with you today.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.540
And we especially want to
thank Dr. Vest and anyone else

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.419
that he might be able
to implicate for having

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.400
the courage to invite us here.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:11.900
[CHEERING]

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.170
Now, I'm sure that--

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.130
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
We figure, this

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.250
is a guy who has an
ironclad contract.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:28.010
I mean, you got to
really have him.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:28.510
Where is he?

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.010
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, I'm sure

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.910
those of you that
know him know that he

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:36.150
is a take-charge,
buck-stops-here kind of guy.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.350
I know that, because
every time his wife Becky

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.050
has taken her automobile
to our shop for repairs,

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.620
he calls personally to
complain about the bill.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.330
And were flattered to find
out, I think just this morning,

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:55.130
that only once before in
the long history of MIT

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.970
has the demand for commencement
tickets been greater.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.990
And coincidentally, it
was when Abraham Lincoln

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.448
spoke to my brother's
graduating class.

00:03:05.448 --> 00:03:07.688
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.900
If anything ever cried
out for an explanation,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.350
it's, why are Tom and
Ray speaking to us today?

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:21.090
And I will attempt, with
the help of my brother,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.930
to give you some kind
of an explanation.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.728
I think you deserve it.

00:03:25.728 --> 00:03:26.770
Are you going to be good?

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.280
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
I'm going to be good.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.238
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
This all started a little

00:03:31.238 --> 00:03:32.070
over two years ago.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.800
We were doing our
weekly radio show.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.340
And I happened to
mention, casually,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.590
that Kofi Annan
had been selected

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.130
to give the address
to the class of '97.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.900
Tommy says, Kofi Annan?

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.055
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Who the hell is he?

00:03:48.055 --> 00:03:50.180
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Whatever happened to Ooh fan?

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.360
And then he begins to rant,
why did they choose Kofi Annan?

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.750
Well, OK, he is the secretary
general of the UN, I guess.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.220
But no one's ever heard of him.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.190
Everyone's heard of us.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.220
They've got to fly him in,
fly him out, put him up

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:10.230
in a fancy hotel, wine him
and dine him and do all that.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.400
They'd have to do none
of these things for us.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.700
And, and, and--

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.950
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: And what?

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:21.514
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: And
he's not even an alumnus.

00:04:21.514 --> 00:04:22.969
[CHEERING]

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.840
Now, I will admit that I did
participate, to some extent,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.760
in his rant and rave.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.580
I've learned, I guess
most of the experts agree,

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.910
that when you're dealing
with these irrational types

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.100
that you shouldn't be
too confrontational.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.080
In fact, you should try
to be a little supportive,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.250
and then hope the
medication kicks in.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:50.320
Well, hardly a fortnight passes,
and we receive in the mail,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.890
from someone named
Charles M. Vest, what

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.303
I would call a terse rebuke.

00:04:56.303 --> 00:04:57.970
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
It wasn't so terse.

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:58.915
I happen to have it here.

00:04:58.915 --> 00:04:59.415
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:59.415 --> 00:05:01.708
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Read it to us, please.

00:05:01.708 --> 00:05:03.250
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Which one is it?

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.637
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
It's the first one.

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:06.970
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Here it is.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.760
Dear Click '58 and Clack '72--

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.060
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Aha,
now you know who we are.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.800
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I was sorry
to learn of your disappointment

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:15.550
at not being--

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:16.860
you don't mind if we read this?

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.936
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: We did
clear this with your office.

00:05:21.936 --> 00:05:23.436
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
What can he do.

00:05:23.436 --> 00:05:25.220
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:26.622 --> 00:05:28.330
I'm sorry to learn of
your disappointment

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.872
at not being asked to deliver
the main address at this year's

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:32.325
commencement exercise.

00:05:32.325 --> 00:05:34.450
It had been my understanding
that you don't usually

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.660
care for exercise,
especially in the open air,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.284
and that you therefore
wouldn't be interested in ours.

00:05:40.284 --> 00:05:41.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.080
On the other hand, as alumnae--

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.437
I'm going with the Greco-Latin
pronunciation here.

00:05:49.437 --> 00:05:51.770
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: I think
Latin would be sufficient.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.450
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: On
the other hand, as alumnae,

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.770
you will appreciate
the fact that we

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:57.050
have some fairly eccentric
students and faculty here

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.340
at the Institute.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.470
So the idea of having
you two gentlemen

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:05.300
as graduation speakers is
invariably floated each spring.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.340
This year, as always, there was
a strong but murky undercurrent

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.480
of support for you as
commencement speakers.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.140
Still, even your
most ardent backers

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.690
had to admit that there was
one crucial area in which

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.730
your qualifications
could not match

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.120
those of your fellow alumnus--

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:22.980
he is an alumnus.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:23.600
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
[CLEARS THROAT]

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:24.660
What does that say?

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.230
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: It says,
of your fellow alumnus, UN

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.570
Secretary General Dag Hammars--

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:32.450
Kofi Annan, '72.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.742
He was a classmate of yours.

00:06:34.742 --> 00:06:36.200
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
No, no, let's

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.367
get this straight right now.

00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:40.850
I was class of '70, '71, '72.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.733
So I couldn't possibly
have known everyone.

00:06:42.733 --> 00:06:43.900
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: No, OK.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:51.290
As you know, the United Nations
has a really spiffy flag.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:54.212
Because Secretary General Annan
was featured as this year's

00:06:54.212 --> 00:06:55.670
speaker, we had a
legitimate excuse

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.730
to fly the UN flag on the
dais, and also to hang it

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:00.710
anywhere else we wanted to.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:02.690
You can imagine how
useful such a flag

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.810
can be when you want to cheer
up a drab corner of the campus

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.630
or decorate a really big
space, like an auditorium

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:10.880
or an athletic cage.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.910
These are the kind of criteria
that this guy Vest is using?

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:17.540
What the hell is he thinking?

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.150
If Car Talk, or even
Dewey, Cheetham, and Howe,

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.162
had possessed a similarly
attractive flag,

00:07:23.162 --> 00:07:24.620
we might have been
able to use you.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.330
But as it was, we
felt that we really

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.260
had to go with the secretary
general for aesthetic reasons.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.460
Right.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.310
You will be pleased to know,
however, that Secretary General

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.470
Annan was a great success.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.230
The graduating seniors
were especially

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.630
moved when he described
his challenge at the UN

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.750
as, a little like try
trying to climb Mt.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:48.920
Washington in a '63 Dodge Dart.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:50.630
He was also warmly
applauded when

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.100
he urged the US
Senate to give him

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:56.690
their share of the gas money
for UN operations worldwide.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.170
Thus, despite your absence,
MIT's '97 commencement

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:01.710
was a smashing success.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.500
Please rest assured
we'll keep you

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.530
in mind for future
ceremonies, blah, blah, blah.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.450
If you ever do get
a flag, let us know.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.173
As you may recall from
your own graduations,

00:08:11.173 --> 00:08:12.590
the participants
want the speakers

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:14.390
to be brief and to the point.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:15.950
I know that brevity
is not regarded

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:17.846
as your most notable quality.

00:08:17.846 --> 00:08:19.867
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:19.867 --> 00:08:21.450
Finally, I would
like to urge you to--

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:22.970
here it comes.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.940
All that for this last,
one-line ending paragraph.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.410
Finally, I would like to
urge you to start sending out

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.030
some really large donations.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.909
Technically yours, Charles M.
Vest, President, blah, blah,

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:38.780
blah, blah, blah.

00:08:41.073 --> 00:08:42.740
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, a whole year

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.750
passes without incident.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.080
Well, I shouldn't
say without incident.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.840
During that year, I think
just about every automaker

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:52.620
on the planet
threatened to sue us.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.970
But at least without incident
with regard to this issue.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:09:02.810
And then last year, Tommy hears
that some elected official--

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:04.670
these are his words, remember--

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:06.313
from Arkansas, who's
been in a little--

00:09:06.313 --> 00:09:07.730
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
I don't think

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.470
I referred to him as
an elected official.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.680
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: --a
little trouble with the law

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.190
is going to give the
address of the class of '98.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:20.540
As you can imagine,
another rant ensues.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.840
Well, it doesn't take
long before Charles M.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.240
Vest puts laser
printer to paper,

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.330
and we receive another rebuke.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.430
I like the sound--
like a Bible story.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:33.680
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Which
I also happen to have here.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.130
Chuck won't mind
if we read this.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:41.420
"Dear Click '58 and Click '72,
I understand that you have,

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.660
once again, expressed
on-air disappointment

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.870
over not being asked to
speak at MIT's graduation.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.090
Last summer, I advised
you that your chances

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.820
of being invited as
commencement speakers

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.560
would be enhanced
if Car Talk had

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.930
a suitable flag
that could be used

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.910
to help us decorate the campus.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:01.677
I hear that you now have
come up with the flag,

00:10:01.677 --> 00:10:03.260
and that you thought
this would assure

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:10.000
your inclusion in the
1998 commencement program.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.040
We went out of our way.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:15.530
If the truth be known, we
got the flag yesterday.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.330
But we told him we had a flag.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.430
We figured he would
be gullible enough--

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.380
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
He went for it.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:22.040
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Certainly it

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.510
is possible that a truly
elegant flag, along with you

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.990
are accountable yet
undeniable popularity

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.450
among your fellow alums
might have gone a long way

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:32.270
toward assuring a place for
you in this year's celebration,

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:34.800
except for two rather
obvious problems.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.600
One, problem number
one, he says,

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.842
you failed to show your
new flag to anyone at MIT.

00:10:42.842 --> 00:10:44.300
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Minor detail.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.675
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Now, I don't

00:10:45.675 --> 00:10:48.000
doubt that the flag exists.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.550
Well, that's not
entirely accurate.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:53.120
I do doubt the flag assists.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.060
But its existence does you no
good if you keep it secret.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.670
There is, after all, a reason
why people use expressions

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:03.000
like, let's run this up the
flagpole, blah, blah, blah.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.540
And the reason is very simply
that people cannot appreciate

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:07.672
your flag unless
they can see it.

00:11:07.672 --> 00:11:10.130
Now, some of my colleagues have
suggested that the Car Talk

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:12.890
flag may be based on
some sort of advanced

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.050
stealth technology,
in which case

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:17.360
I applaud your
technical prowess.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.100
On the whole, however,
a stealth flag

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:20.960
seems to be a
self-defeating innovation.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.940
And he's right about that.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:26.660
Problem number two, one of this
year's commencement speakers

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.390
is the Honorable William
Jefferson Clinton, President

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:31.850
of these here United States.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:35.270
I paraphrased there.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.910
As the duly elected leader of
the world's only superpower,

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.580
Mr. Clinton not only
comes with his own flag,

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.380
and a darn good one, too.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.780
But he's even got a seal.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:46.620
Talk about upping the ante.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.390
Heck, this guy brings his own
podium, audience, press corps,

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.350
helicopter, you name it.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.370
There's just no way for the two
of you to compete this year.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.050
You think you're disappointed?

00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:59.270
Not even I get to make
a speech this year,

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.011
and I'm supposed
to run the place.

00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:03.120
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.730
Nonetheless, he says, I urge
you not to give up hope.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:10.910
Send us pictures of
this alleged flag,

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.560
along with appropriate
contributions to the alumni

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.060
fund.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.920
Jeez, will the
guy never give up?

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:17.720
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: He's
like a broken record, isn't he?

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.012
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Good luck.

00:12:19.012 --> 00:12:23.450
I remain technically
yours, Charles M. Vest.

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:25.200
We got another
letter, by the way.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:26.950
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
I'm getting to that.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.325
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Oh, you are?

00:12:28.325 --> 00:12:29.908
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Don't interrupt.

00:12:29.908 --> 00:12:31.370
After these two
letters, of course,

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:35.300
Tommy continues to rant,
albeit sporadically.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.900
He says, because we
haven't been chosen

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:42.500
and been overlooked by MIT,
he's lost face and credibility.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.530
You'd want to lose a face
like this, wouldn't you?

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.950
Friends and neighbors have
turned a cold shoulder to him.

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:50.940
His wife and kids have
lost respect for him

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.670
and barely talk to him.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.300
Even the dog won't play with
him unless he has a pork chop

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.590
tied around his neck.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.950
Then in the spring of this
year, inexplicably, we

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:04.670
receive a beautifully written,
I would say almost conciliatory,

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:09.980
letter from Dr. Vest asking
us to speak to you today.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.790
Of course, we lost that letter.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.280
We don't know where it is.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.840
But I do know that
Dr. Vest figured

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:20.870
that we would have the dignity,
the good sense, and the respect

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:26.750
for MIT to refuse, and to
realize that the invitation was

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.880
a joke.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:31.490
Negative on all counts.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:36.250
And of course, we become
immediately suspicious.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.740
We start to think,
how many others were

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.810
asked before they got to us?

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.920
I mean, after all, we knew--

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:44.470
we're not kidding anyone--

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:47.440
we are at the
bottom of the heap.

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:49.670
Exactly right, we're at
the bottom of the heap.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:52.280
So they must have asked
thousands of other people.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:54.640
So we engage our
crack detective,

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:58.120
Paul Murky of Murky
Investigations,

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:04.450
to find out who was asked, and
the reason he or she refused.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:05.700
I have the list.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:06.610
Just stand back.

00:14:09.907 --> 00:14:12.262
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:14.075 --> 00:14:16.450
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: These
are the requests that he made?

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:17.533
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Yes.

00:14:17.533 --> 00:14:19.300
Now, due to certain
treaty restrictions

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:22.930
and other strict
protocols, and time,

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:24.440
we can't read all the names.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.310
But we do have a few that
we're allowed to read.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:31.000
And I will give you the name
of the person who was asked

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:32.050
and the reason.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.300
My brother will give you the
reason he or she refused.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.633
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I've
memorized the whole pile here.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:42.680
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
OK, Kofi Annan.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:46.990
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Yeah, he's
said, been there, done that.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:49.090
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Here's one.

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:50.700
Oh, a duo--

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:52.800
Pons and Fleischmann.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.380
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Yeah, they
said, very low energy level.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.957
We can't do it.

00:14:57.957 --> 00:15:00.290
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Oh, one
of my personal favorites--

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.150
Leonid Brezhnev.

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:03.740
I love saying his name.

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:04.730
Can I say it again?

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.230
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Is it like Arup Gupta?

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:07.355
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Yeah.

00:15:07.355 --> 00:15:08.083
Leonid Brezhnev.

00:15:08.083 --> 00:15:09.500
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Yeah, he said

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:11.083
he couldn't do it
because he was dead.

00:15:13.247 --> 00:15:14.580
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Let's see.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.177
Oh, yes, Dolly the cloned sheep.

00:15:17.177 --> 00:15:19.260
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: She
couldn't come because she

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.200
didn't have FDA approval.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:28.310
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: And last
but not least, Leonid Brezhnev.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:31.040
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Still dead.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.690
So then he got to us, I guess.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:34.065
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
I guess so.

00:15:34.065 --> 00:15:37.220
But in spite of this news, we're
still excited and thrilled,

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:40.470
of course, and
honored to be here.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:44.750
And after the
euphoria subsided, it

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.430
began to sink in
that we actually

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:49.130
had to give a speech today.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:50.740
And I will admit
that I was concerned

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:52.550
to maybe even a bit worried.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:55.340
After all, commencement
speeches are usually

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:59.180
reserved for heads of
state, respected members

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:02.370
of the academic
community, secretaries

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:03.620
general of the United Nations.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:05.660
But us, why us?

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:09.860
But my fears began to ebb as
I weighed the consequences

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.760
of a poor performance today.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:13.770
What if we do terribly?

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:19.575
What if we're incoherent,
uninspiring, uninteresting?

00:16:19.575 --> 00:16:22.230
It'd be just like
our radio show.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.700
I mean, what could
possibly happen?

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:28.163
What could they do, ask
for our diplomas back?

00:16:28.163 --> 00:16:29.580
They couldn't do
that, could they?

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.290
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
I don't think so.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.270
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: I don't
want to give my diploma back.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:33.990
I can't.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.657
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
No, it's holding up

00:16:35.657 --> 00:16:37.780
the end of that table
in your dining room.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:39.780
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, after this epiphany,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:43.580
I began to feel a
lightness of being.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:46.920
And Tom and I rolled
up our sleeves,

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:51.810
put on a pot of coffee, and
began the creative process.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.750
Now, he'll devolve the
intricacies of that process

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.400
soon enough-- but
first, the warnings.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.280
I have to move this.

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:06.675
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:14.555
What an interesting perspective.

00:17:14.555 --> 00:17:15.930
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
That's good.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.680
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
You know, my brother

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:20.650
frequently makes what I would
call offensive and insulting

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:21.700
statements.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:27.040
So my main function here today,
other than introducing him,

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:32.620
is to, number one, deflect
any hurled fruit, number two,

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:36.410
to try to prevent him from
insulting any religious,

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:42.560
ethnic, or paramilitary groups,
to quell any civil unrest that

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.780
may result from anything
he says or does,

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:48.740
and to interrupt and clarify,
and say things like, well, he

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:53.300
didn't mean to say that about
every member of the faculty.

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:56.210
So without further
ado, I would like

00:17:56.210 --> 00:18:00.090
to introduce my esteemed
brother, Professor Thomas

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.112
Magliozzi.

00:18:01.112 --> 00:18:01.612
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:01.612 --> 00:18:03.195
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
That would be me.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:08.637
Well, we had to use
technology, actually.

00:18:08.637 --> 00:18:09.970
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: May I sit.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.390
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Aw, jeez.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:15.910
We figured doing this at the
world's foremost institute

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:18.460
of technology on
Massachusetts Avenue,

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:21.350
and so we ought
to use technology.

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.180
So what my brother
did actually was,

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:26.440
he requested, from Paul
Parravano, who's, I guess,

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:28.960
the vice president of MIT.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.730
Where is Paul?

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:31.470
We don't know.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.178
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
They threw him out.

00:18:33.178 --> 00:18:35.980
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: He
requested copies of the last 20

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:40.050
years commencement addresses.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:43.920
We gave this to our
crack researcher,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.890
Paul Murky of Murky
Research, brother of the Paul

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:49.920
Murky of Murky Investigations.

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:55.560
And we asked him to analyze
all of these speeches

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.800
to find out if there
were some commonalities.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.690
And indeed, there were.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.820
He used factor analysis,
which, of course,

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.800
15 guys will understand.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:10.520
And he came up
with three factors.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:11.770
Well, get this.

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:17.080
Every one of these speeches
had in common a beginning,

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:20.485
a middle, and an end.

00:19:20.485 --> 00:19:21.985
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Wow, powerful.

00:19:24.593 --> 00:19:26.260
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, this is good.

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:29.140
Because we were so
thrilled at this,

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:32.110
that we had made such
progress, that we put it

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:34.015
aside for about a month.

00:19:34.015 --> 00:19:35.895
We didn't have to
think about it anymore.

00:19:35.895 --> 00:19:38.520
Then we looked at it one day and
said, this is a little skimpy.

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:43.010
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
I hate to interrupt.

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:45.670
But the good news is, our wives
are not hanging their heads

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:46.518
in shame yet.

00:19:46.518 --> 00:19:47.810
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: They will.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:49.653
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
OK, go ahead.

00:19:49.653 --> 00:19:51.820
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: We
figured, the beginning, that

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:53.330
was pretty straightforward.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:56.170
We could read a couple of
letters from Chuck Vest.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:20:00.310
So we did that already,
and so that's done.

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:02.152
The middle, that
was the tough part.

00:20:02.152 --> 00:20:04.360
We couldn't quite figure
what the middle ought to be.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.910
But the end, we knew,
had to be some kind

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:09.850
of inspirational thing.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.010
That's what they all are.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:13.840
So we said to ourselves,
what do we know

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.540
about inspirational things?

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:19.850
It so happens, however--

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:24.010
man, it so happens that
we have, in addition

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:28.210
to Murky Investigations,
Paul Murky,

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.640
whom I just told you
did the factor analysis,

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.540
had been working on some
other research for us.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:37.990
And he and his lovely
assistant Marge Innovera--

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.488
Marge Innovera.

00:20:42.488 --> 00:20:44.530
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Yeah, they got it, I think.

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:45.760
It just wasn't that funny.

00:20:49.172 --> 00:20:51.130
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I'll
tell you the genesis.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:55.180
It's interesting how
research happens.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.370
One day, I can't
remember which one of us

00:20:57.370 --> 00:21:02.800
it was, had come
across, interestingly--

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:03.700
isn't it interesting?

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:07.660
I mean, coincidence-- we just
had a little Hebrew prayer.

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:10.690
And one of us had
thought of a quotation

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:16.976
from one of another great Hebrew
philosopher, Isaac Newton.

00:21:16.976 --> 00:21:18.912
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:21.333
And if you recall--

00:21:21.333 --> 00:21:23.000
which you probably
don't, because you're

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:24.800
a bunch of nerds who
only think in numbers.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:26.050
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Stop it.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:26.690
Behave.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:29.232
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: He said,
if I have accomplished anything

00:21:29.232 --> 00:21:33.290
in my life, it is
because I have stood

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:37.352
on the shoulders of giants.

00:21:37.352 --> 00:21:39.330
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Wow.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.850
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
That's what we said, wow.

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:44.490
But after a few moments,
we thought about it a bit

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.700
and said, it's one of
those things-- you know,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.600
like George Carlin, he
says, sounds good, but--

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:57.150
and we wondered, is it in fact
true in all areas of endeavor

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:00.420
that people accomplish great
things because they stand

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:02.950
on the shoulders of giants?

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:09.300
So we tell Paul Murky,
do some research on this.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:14.310
And he comes back to us
with a couple of hypotheses.

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:16.590
He's a great
researcher, this man.

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:19.620
The null hypothesis
is, well, of course

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.500
it applies to all areas
and all endeavors.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.410
Because we, as humans,
have been on the planet

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.660
for hundreds of
thousands of years.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:31.890
And we each benefit
from whatever has

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:35.000
been done by our predecessors.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:38.270
That's the shoulders
of giants hypothesis.

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:42.300
The alternative
hypothesis is, oh, yeah?

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:47.770
The alternative hypothesis
is, it's not true.

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:52.430
In some areas, yes,
we do, in fact,

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:54.510
benefit from what our
predecessors have done.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:23:01.160
But in other areas, mostly
the human involvement kinds

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.220
of areas, we may,
in fact, all be

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:08.690
destined to make the same
mistakes over and over

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:13.350
and over again, generation after
generation, child after child.

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:18.560
And so there is never any
giant on whose shoulders

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:19.940
you could stand.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:22.910
And therefore, there
is no progress.

00:23:22.910 --> 00:23:26.880
There is an interesting
set of hypotheses.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:32.100
This one is called the "oh
yeah" alternative hypothesis.

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:35.630
So Murky goes out
and starts to work.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:42.500
And as he will do, Murky
doesn't stick strictly

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:43.850
to what we asked him to do.

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:46.640
And one day, we
catch him, I think

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:50.090
it's called mucking
around in the data.

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:52.670
And sure enough, he
comes to us and says,

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:57.380
guys, I have been mucking
around in the data.

00:23:57.380 --> 00:24:01.250
And I have a
finding here that is

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:04.050
going to knock your socks off.

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:08.400
And I have to apologize
for our visual aids here.

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:12.630
I did prepare a complete
PowerPoint presentation.

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.680
And I asked for an
overhead projector,

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:16.530
and they couldn't find one here.

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:17.905
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
No, I think

00:24:17.905 --> 00:24:19.980
they told him to drop dead.

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:24.420
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Could I
have a slide number two please?

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:25.510
Come on, Chuck.

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:26.670
Earn your buck here.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.260
Earn your money, number two.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.593
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: What's
the matter with number one?

00:24:30.593 --> 00:24:33.855
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I
already used that one.

00:24:33.855 --> 00:24:35.340
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:36.145 --> 00:24:38.062
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Should we turn it around

00:24:38.062 --> 00:24:39.800
so people behind us can see?

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:41.140
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Yeah, just
turn it around for a minute.

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:42.890
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
We'll be right back.

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:44.155
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:49.920
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Don't
fall off the stage, Chuck.

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:53.790
Now, those in the back
with the cheap seats,

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:54.730
you can't see this.

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:56.580
But it's very straightforward.

00:24:56.580 --> 00:24:58.080
I may have to move
over to this mic.

00:24:58.080 --> 00:24:59.310
Does this mic work?

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:02.240
Yes, it does.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:07.260
Murky says to us, I've plotted
something interesting here.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.830
We're talking about, basically,
left brain versus right brain

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:11.940
function.

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:14.010
He says, and while
mucking around,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.990
I find this interesting
relationship.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:18.700
This is left brain on this end.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:20.725
This is a right
brain on the right.

00:25:23.377 --> 00:25:25.210
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
And what's the y-axis?

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:27.127
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Well, I'll tell you that.

00:25:27.127 --> 00:25:28.090
This is a plot--

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:33.010
he did a regression analysis on
this with an R square of 0.99

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.350
and a significance of 0.001.

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:36.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:36.970 --> 00:25:44.642
And this, this
axis is happiness.

00:25:44.642 --> 00:25:45.960
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Whoa.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.930
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Doesn't
that knock your socks off?

00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:54.010
He says, left
brain, right brain.

00:25:54.010 --> 00:26:02.050
And if you think about it, this
is sort of what we think of--

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:04.690
some people do at
least-- intelligence.

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:08.740
So it's almost a plot of
intelligence versus happiness.

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.038
And the news ain't good for you.

00:26:11.038 --> 00:26:13.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:16.540
Because what Murky finds out
is that right-brained people

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:20.860
are about 10 times as happy
as left-brained people.

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:23.490
So the stupider you get--

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.470
by left-brained
people's measures

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:28.420
of stupidity, of course.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:31.030
Because right-brained people
are too happy to waste

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:33.940
their time developing IQ tests.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.550
But they're 10 times happier.

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:40.975
We say, woof, Paul,
this is something.

00:26:40.975 --> 00:26:43.330
But that's not the end of it.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:47.020
Because we do what any
good researcher would do.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:49.300
We want to extrapolate.

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:51.780
And do we want to extrapolate
in this direction?

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:52.933
Hell, no.

00:26:52.933 --> 00:26:53.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:56.870
We want to extrapolate
in that direction.

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:58.870
So we say to Paul,
if this really

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:02.430
is intelligence going
in that direction, what

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:04.990
we need is dumber people.

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:08.230
Let's see if it goes on and on.

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:11.220
So where do you go?

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:12.240
He goes to Harvard.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.132
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.500
He comes back to us
about a month later.

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:25.730
And he says, it
just isn't working.

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:29.463
He said, they're
not dumb enough.

00:27:29.463 --> 00:27:31.630
And I say, what do you mean
they're not dumb enough?

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:36.100
They don't get any dumber
than Harvard students.

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:39.170
And he says, people
don't get any dumber

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:41.240
than Harvard students.

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:46.428
But why do we have to limit
our research to people?

00:27:46.428 --> 00:27:48.220
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Can we put this down?

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:49.470
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Oh, sure.

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:50.620
Are you still there.

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:51.370
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:56.870
I'm just trying to drive the
guy with the microphones crazy.

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:00.530
No, don't go away.

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:01.905
We're going to
need number three.

00:28:01.905 --> 00:28:03.400
We're going to
need number three.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.980
He says, I can
extend the research

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:09.550
to include other life forms.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:13.840
And through a methodology,
which he will not reveal to us,

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:19.210
he was able to determine the
happiness level of other life

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:19.900
forms.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:21.390
Slide three, please.

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:26.380
Three, slide three.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:27.240
Here it is.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:29.940
Here's humans.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:32.740
And the best of
humans, of course,

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:34.680
is the right-brained humans.

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:38.340
And here is what he found.

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:40.020
Happiness goes up.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:44.930
It begins to look like it's
exponential over there.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:48.980
The next happier life form
is a golden retriever.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:50.944
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:59.605
Then a cow, then worms, and he
stopped his research at grass.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.440
You can turn that to show the
faculty, because they may not

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:07.250
understand what
I'm talking about.

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:10.630
Now--

00:29:13.987 --> 00:29:15.987
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: OK,
we'll just keep going.

00:29:18.747 --> 00:29:20.330
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Here's the story.

00:29:20.330 --> 00:29:23.770
What is the importance of this?

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:27.560
We have always thought that
we were the highest life

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:30.582
form on the planet.

00:29:30.582 --> 00:29:35.640
It turns out we are the lowest
life form on the planet.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:39.870
And I am going to give to
you now a theorem which

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:43.520
will knock your socks off.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:49.910
Some theories you know are
just complete bullshit--

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:53.960
for example, the
Big Bang Theory?

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:55.202
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:56.030 --> 00:30:03.480
The entire universe is
compacted into a dot.

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:05.970
It explodes.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:07.050
Why?

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:14.400
Well, they don't know why, so
they call it a singularity.

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:18.480
That's like a bimbo
saying, well, it just did.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:19.830
It explodes.

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.710
And out of it come
all the stars that you

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:28.860
can see in the sky, all
the planets, Madonna--

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:34.580
[LAUGHTER]---- corned
beef sandwiches.

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:38.780
Now, if you didn't hear
that and say, oh, come on--

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.720
but I am going to
give you the theorem,

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:48.110
and you're going to say,
why didn't I think of that?

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:50.790
What does all of this tell us?

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:52.740
That we are not the
highest life form.

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:59.480
This is the theory of
reverse incarnation.

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:02.150
Some people believe
in reincarnation.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:05.630
And what they believe
is that, when we die,

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:10.970
we come back as better
and better people.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:13.220
What the theory of
reverse reincarnation

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:16.720
says, if we are good
people, we will come back

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:17.980
as a golden retriever--

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:25.625
[LAUGHTER]---- then a cow,
then a worm, then grass.

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:33.270
Now, if the reincarnation was
working in the other direction,

00:31:33.270 --> 00:31:37.207
coming back as better and
better people, where are they?

00:31:37.207 --> 00:31:39.195
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:40.640
Duh.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:48.570
So it becomes clear that the
theory of reverse reincarnation

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:53.880
may be the scientific finding
of, not the decade, not the

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:56.820
century, but of all time.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:02.240
Now, my brother and
I, L. Ron Magliozzi,

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:07.450
are going to help you
to achieve nirvana.

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:12.690
We're going to help you to
get to become not smarter--

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:14.160
smarter is no good.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:15.585
That's the wrong direction.

00:32:15.585 --> 00:32:17.460
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
You've been doing that.

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:20.790
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: You have
spent the last four, five,

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:26.910
or six years of your life
working on the wrong direction.

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:30.150
You are sliding down, as Tom
Lehrer says, sliding down

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.100
the razor blade of life.

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:34.530
You are sliding down
the happiness curve.

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:37.560
You must stop this
from happening,

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.300
and you must go in
the other direction.

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:42.660
And we are here to help you.

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:49.650
And as you know, there is a
process for reaching nirvana,

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.840
and we are going to
give it to you now.

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:54.000
It is this.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.990
You must repeat the mantra.

00:32:56.990 --> 00:33:02.070
And the mantra, which happens
to be emblazoned on our flag,

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:04.170
which stands here--

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.420
none of you morons
will be able to read it

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:07.838
because it's in Latin.

00:33:07.838 --> 00:33:09.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:13.750
It says, non impediti
ratione cogitatonis.

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:15.660
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Which of course means,

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.480
unencumbered by the
thought process.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:19.390
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:21.210
[CHEERING]

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:29.280
Now, I am going to give you
a very brief history of how

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:31.652
this mantra has helped me.

00:33:31.652 --> 00:33:33.110
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
I can't wait.

00:33:35.503 --> 00:33:36.920
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
If you repeat

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:45.310
this mantra, what happens
is, everything slows down.

00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:47.590
Life slows down.

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:50.260
Being unencumbered by
the thought process

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:59.230
allows you to identify and
hear and see defining moments

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:02.020
in your life, things that
will change your life.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.179
Unencumbered by the
thought process--

00:34:08.179 --> 00:34:11.000
you say it over and over again.

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:16.130
And as everything slows
down and begins to stop,

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:19.310
we call these, by the
way, moments of inertia.

00:34:19.310 --> 00:34:20.134
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:20.134 --> 00:34:23.750
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Oh, god, is that bad.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:26.030
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I
had to use it, though.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:28.888
I had to.

00:34:28.888 --> 00:34:35.350
I was once trapped by
the scientific logic,

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:38.110
left-brain life.

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:42.909
I graduated from here, and I
went to work as an engineer.

00:34:42.909 --> 00:34:47.860
And I will tell you
about my defining moment.

00:34:47.860 --> 00:34:49.570
I was driving.

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:50.980
I lived in Cambridge
at the time.

00:34:50.980 --> 00:34:53.230
I was driving from
Cambridge to my job

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:55.739
in Foxboro, Massachusetts.

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:58.735
And I was driving
in a little MG.

00:34:58.735 --> 00:35:01.930
It weighed about 50 pounds.

00:35:01.930 --> 00:35:08.970
And on Route 128, I
was cut off by a semi.

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:13.360
And I almost, as they
say, bought the farm.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:19.550
And as I continued my
drive, I said to myself,

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:23.620
if I had, in fact, bought the
farm out there on Route 128,

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:29.470
how ticked off would I be that
I had spent all of my life,

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:33.220
that I can remember, at
least, going to this job,

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:36.790
living a life of
quiet desperation?

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:40.510
So I pulled into
the parking lot,

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:42.640
walked into my boss's office.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.460
And I quit on the spot.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:46.210
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
See now, most people

00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:47.710
would have just
bought a bigger car.

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:49.130
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: [LAUGHS]

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:50.758
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
So act now.

00:35:50.758 --> 00:35:52.050
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Yeah, see?

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:55.840
But those people would have
been using their left brains.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.720
I had been saying my
mantra in the car.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:35:59.980
That's why the guy cut me off.

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:01.960
I think I cut him off.

00:36:01.960 --> 00:36:04.450
In any event, I quit my job.

00:36:04.450 --> 00:36:06.070
I became a bum.

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:10.600
I spent two years sitting in
Harvard Square drinking coffee.

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:13.690
I invented the concept of the
do-it-yourself auto repair

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:19.630
shop, and I met my lovely
wife, none of which

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:24.100
would have happened if I had
been using my left brain.

00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:27.310
My second great
defining moment came,

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:30.850
also showing the
power of the mantra,

00:36:30.850 --> 00:36:34.240
unencumbered by the
thought process.

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:37.120
I was having an argument
with my lovely wife one day.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.640
I mean, how can you argue
with such a wonderful person?

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:42.790
Well, left-brained
people do that.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:46.300
Because all they can think
of is, this is an argument.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:48.700
This person is over
here, and I'm over here.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:52.450
And I am going to use every
ounce of logic and skill

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:58.120
that I have so that I
can win this argument.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:37:01.060
And my wife says
to me, do you want

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:06.030
to be right or do
you want to be happy?

00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:07.835
Holy shit, says I.

00:37:07.835 --> 00:37:09.655
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:10.565 --> 00:37:13.310
I wanted to be happy.

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:17.110
So now I have reached nirvana.

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:19.330
And my brother and I can
help you to reach it,

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:21.790
if you want to repeat after me.

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:24.250
Unencumbered by the
thought process.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:24.910
Say it.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:27.360
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: You
may have to stand for this.

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:29.110
RAYMOND AND THOMAS
MAGLIOZZI: Unencumbered

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:30.418
by the thought process.

00:37:30.418 --> 00:37:31.960
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Louder, come on.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:36.430
Unencumbered by the
thought process!

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:37.450
One more time.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:42.740
Unencumbered by the
thought process!

00:37:42.740 --> 00:37:44.800
Follow us, my
children, to happiness.

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:46.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:48.107
Are there any questions?

00:37:48.107 --> 00:37:50.055
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:50.937 --> 00:37:52.270
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Thank you.

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:53.650
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
Are you happy?

00:37:53.650 --> 00:37:53.890
I'm

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:55.140
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Excited.

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:58.570
Thank you, Professor Wagstaff.

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:00.190
That was most informative.

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:05.550
That does remind me of a
famous Latin expression.

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:06.930
[SPEAKING LATIN]

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:14.520
Which means, if
Caesar were alive,

00:38:14.520 --> 00:38:17.274
you'd be chained to an oar.

00:38:17.274 --> 00:38:19.142
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:24.272
Look, we won't belabor this
any more than we have to.

00:38:24.272 --> 00:38:25.980
But this is the part
of the address where

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:29.970
we're supposed to say
something meaningful

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:32.713
and impart some words of wisdom.

00:38:32.713 --> 00:38:34.380
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
What did I just do?

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:37.120
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
We're not sure.

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:39.930
But I'm not sure that we're
in possession of any wisdom,

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:42.730
but we've never
let that stop us.

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:43.450
So listen up.

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:45.075
I'm only going to
say this a few times.

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:49.650
Today you will
receive a document

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:53.420
that states that you've earned
a degree, or maybe degrees,

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.040
from MIT.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:59.880
You've worked hard, and you
should feel a great sense

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:01.850
of accomplishment.

00:39:01.850 --> 00:39:04.528
I know I did.

00:39:04.528 --> 00:39:06.070
And most of you will
leave here today

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.380
with a pretty good idea
of where you're going

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:11.680
and what you're going to do.

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:15.390
Some of you have no clue.

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:18.120
And you'll just have to move
back in with your parents,

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:21.480
if they haven't rented
out your room already.

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:22.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:22.830 --> 00:39:26.670
But others among you may
have chartered a course

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:31.180
or had one chartered for
you that you know is wrong.

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:34.620
And you may feel some
creative energy coursing

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:36.510
through your body.

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:38.250
Don't ignore it.

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:41.112
If you feel the urge
to create and discover

00:39:41.112 --> 00:39:42.570
and to do something
that will bring

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:49.110
you fulfillment and happiness,
do it now while you're young.

00:39:49.110 --> 00:39:57.510
You will never have more energy
or enthusiasm, hair, or brain

00:39:57.510 --> 00:39:59.730
cells than you have today.

00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:01.710
Do you know, when
Albert Einstein was

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:04.470
less than half my
age, he was already

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:08.640
world famous for his special
theory of relativity?

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:12.750
When Isaac Newton was
less than half my age,

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:15.660
he was already famous for
having invented calculus.

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:18.660
And he pretty much had written
your entire 801 physics

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:21.150
textbook.

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:24.840
Mozart when he was
half my brother's age

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:26.340
had been dead for three years.

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:28.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:29.420
So act now.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:36.758
Lastly, I've skipped a
bunch of things because--

00:40:36.758 --> 00:40:38.050
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Thank God.

00:40:40.085 --> 00:40:41.460
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
I just want

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:45.570
to encourage you to never get so
involved in your work, whatever

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:49.920
it is, that you
forget to have fun.

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:52.290
You know, my brother
may be right.

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:57.360
If you're lucky, you may come
back as a schnauzer or a bichon

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.680
frise.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:00.360
But you may not.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:02.540
With all this spaying
and neutering going on,

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:06.210
the odds are getting worse
and worse every year.

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:09.280
So have fun now
and enjoy yourself.

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:12.120
And I'd like to leave
you with some words

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:16.858
of a wise man, a wise man from
the east my brother and I know.

00:41:16.858 --> 00:41:18.150
All wise men are from the east.

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:21.037
Have you ever met a wise man
from the west or the middle?

00:41:21.037 --> 00:41:22.120
They're all from the east.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:24.700
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: I think
Spielberg's from the west.

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:27.300
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: And
this wise man is no exception,

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:29.235
except he's from East Boston.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:37.357
And his name is
Depak Fonzarelli.

00:41:37.357 --> 00:41:38.940
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
He's quite a man.

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:39.990
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
He is quite a man.

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:41.600
And Tommy and I go
to him from time

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:48.090
to time to learn the answers
to weighty questions,

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:51.210
like, what is the
meaning of life?

00:41:51.210 --> 00:41:56.100
What are next week's winning
lottery numbers going to be?

00:41:56.100 --> 00:42:00.360
And we went to him recently,
and Tommy and I sat with him.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:04.440
And Tommy asked him how he
could attain immortality.

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:10.122
Depak sat for a minute.

00:42:10.122 --> 00:42:13.830
He got up and turned off the TV.

00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:15.290
Baywatch had just ended.

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:23.380
And he said, my son, if you
wish to attain immortality,

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.030
you must do the following.

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:29.720
You must work hard every
day, seven days a week,

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:32.190
never taking time off.

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:33.990
You must the attend
no social functions.

00:42:33.990 --> 00:42:38.090
You must not smoke,
you must not drink,

00:42:38.090 --> 00:42:41.000
and you must not go with women.

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:43.520
Never had we received
such a definitive answer

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:45.740
to any of our questions.

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:46.670
We were astounded.

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:51.100
And Tommy asked--

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:52.600
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI:
I say to Depak,

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:58.670
you mean if I do those
things, I will live forever?

00:42:58.670 --> 00:43:00.850
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI:
Oh, no, my son, he said,

00:43:00.850 --> 00:43:02.350
it will just seem like forever.

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:04.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:08.798
Have fun, enjoy the ride, and
don't drive like my brother.

00:43:08.798 --> 00:43:09.465
Congratulations.

00:43:09.465 --> 00:43:11.080
THOMAS F. MAGLIOZZI: Don't
drive like my brother.

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:12.413
RAYMOND L. MAGLIOZZI: Thank you.

00:43:12.413 --> 00:43:14.640
[APPLAUSE]

